## What the experiment says as it runs: its steps, board and answer. These texts come with the experiment when it opens; its card is in scales-diy.ftl.

scales-diy-command-apply = No such step in the experiment.
scales-diy-zero-say = The scales are ready: a ruler hung by a thread at its middle, a yoghurt cup at each end. But the empty cups do not balance: one is a little heavier. We stick a bit of plasticine to the lighter one — and pinch it off until the ruler is level.
scales-diy-zero-button = ⚖ Even out the empty cups
scales-diy-zero-plan = even out the empty cups
scales-diy-check-say = We check the scales. A sheet of A4 office paper (80 g a square metre) weighs 5 g — we fold it and put it in the left cup. Into the right one we squirt water from a syringe: 1 mL of water is 1 g. How many millilitres will it take?
scales-diy-check-button = 📄 A sheet of A4 against water
scales-diy-check-plan = a sheet of A4 against water from a syringe
scales-diy-egg-say = Now a hen’s egg. As with weights: the big ones first — sheets of 5 g while the egg outweighs them; then the small ones — water from the syringe, a millilitre at a time, until the ruler is level.
scales-diy-egg-button = 🥚 Weigh the egg
scales-diy-egg-plan = weigh the egg with sheets and water
scales-diy-answer-why =
    The ruler’s arms are equal, and the Earth pulls equal masses equally — so the ruler stays level when the masses in the cups are equal. Anything of known mass can be a weight: a sheet of paper, water from a syringe. As with a set of weights, the big ones go first, then the small.

    Scales are among the oldest instruments, and they were long trusted as the fairest of judges: the arms are equal, neither pan has the advantage. In the ancient Egyptian Book of the Dead (its scrolls were written from about 1550 to 50 BC) a person’s heart is laid on the scales against the feather of Maat — the goddess of truth and justice. The god Anubis weighs, the god Thoth writes it down, and if the scales balance, the person lived honestly.

    And weights were once checked the same way — with water. In 1795 the gram was defined as the mass of a cubic centimetre of water. In 1889 the world’s chief weight became Le Grand K — a cylinder of platinum and iridium 39 mm tall, kept near Paris. Its copies were compared with it on scales — and over a century they drifted apart by about 50 micrograms. So since 20 May 2019 the kilogram has been defined by the Planck constant and weighed on Kibble balances: there an electric current takes the place of the weight.
scales-diy-answer-say = The egg — { $value }, and not a single weight!
scales-diy-text-parts = The empty cups were evened with plasticine.
scales-diy-answer-text = The A4 sheet was balanced by { $water } mL of water — { $value }: the sheet does weigh { $value2 }, the scales agree with themselves.
scales-diy-answer-text-2 = The egg: { $sheets } sheets of 5 g and { $water } mL of water — { $value }. The scales feel about half a gram, so they weigh no better than to a gram.
